# Components and injection

Start with the value your application needs, then make its dependencies fields. The <Symbol path="upwell::prelude::component" label="#[component]" /> attribute turns that type into a registered, singleton-scoped component whose ordinary fields are resolved from the container.

<Example title="Build a component from injected fields">

```rust
use std::sync::Arc;
use upwell::prelude::*;

#[component]
struct Database;

impl Database {
    async fn search(&self, query: &str) -> Vec<String> {
        vec![query.into()]
    }
}

#[derive(Default)]
struct QueryCache;
```

Make the application value a component. `Arc<Database>` is injected; `#[default]` keeps local state out of the dependency graph.

```rust
#[component]
struct Catalog {
    database: Arc<Database>,
    #[default]
    cache: QueryCache,
}
```

Use the constructed component normally. The container creates it once and shares that instance with its consumers.

```rust
impl Catalog {
    async fn search(&self, query: &str) -> Vec<String> {
        let _ = &self.cache;
        self.database.search(query).await
    }
}
```

</Example>

Use this shape when fields already describe construction. Fields can also express optional dependencies, a selected trait provider, a collection of providers, or a configuration binding. Choose the smallest handle that matches the component's contract; the [advanced DI guide](advanced) helps with cases that need live or delayed values.

## Use a constructor for setup work

Move construction into `#[init]` when initialization needs I/O, validation, derived state, or can fail. `#[methods]` registers the constructor, which replaces the generated field factory.

<Example title="Connect during construction">

```rust
use std::sync::Arc;
use upwell::prelude::*;

#[component]
struct Database;

impl Database {
    async fn open_connection(&self) -> Result<usize, AppError> {
        Ok(1)
    }
}
```

Declare the finished component. Its fields are implementation details rather than injection instructions here.

```rust
#[component]
struct DatabaseConnection {
    id: usize,
}
```

Accept dependencies in an initializer and return the completed value.

```rust
#[methods]
impl DatabaseConnection {
    #[init]
    async fn connect(database: Arc<Database>) -> Result<Self, AppError> {
        Ok(Self {
            id: database.open_connection().await?,
        })
    }
}
```

</Example>

The initializer can be synchronous or asynchronous and can return either the component directly or a `Result`. Use `#[hook(...)]` in the same `#[methods]` implementation for lifecycle behavior that runs after construction. See the [methods symbol page](/docs/latest/symbols/upwell_macros/methods) for hook and initializer signatures.

## Depend on behavior, not an implementation

When consumers need a capability rather than one concrete type, mark the object-safe trait `#[injectable]`, expose a component through `provide`, and inject the trait object.

<Example title="Provide and select a trait implementation">

```rust
use std::sync::Arc;
use upwell::prelude::*;

#[injectable]
trait AuditSink: Send + Sync {
    fn write(&self, event: &str);
}
```

Register a concrete implementation as the named provider for that capability.

```rust
#[component(provide = dyn AuditSink, qualifier = "file")]
struct FileAuditSink;

impl AuditSink for FileAuditSink {
    fn write(&self, event: &str) {
        eprintln!("{event}");
    }
}
```

Ask for that provider where it is used. A qualifier chooses a particular implementation.

```rust
#[component]
struct Checkout {
    #[qualifier = "file"]
    audit: Arc<dyn AuditSink>,
}

impl Checkout {
    fn complete(&self) {
        self.audit.write("checkout completed");
    }
}
```

</Example>

Injecting both the concrete component and its trait view still uses one constructed instance. Use an unqualified trait dependency when one provider is available or a provider is marked primary; use a collection only when every provider should participate.

